         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we learned to write Etruscan-Roman numbers from 10 to 999,999:\n- In the tens, the unit is X and the middle value is L; four tens are written XL and nine tens are written XC.\n- In the hundreds, the unit is C and the middle value is D; four hundreds are written CD and nine hundreds are written CM.\n- In the thousands, a bar above a group of symbols multiplies its value by one thousand; with this resource we can write all the numbers up to 999,999.\n\nThe pattern of the digits from 1 to 9 repeats at every level, changing only the symbols: I, V and X for the units; X, L and C for the tens; C, D and M for the hundreds; and, above one thousand, the same symbols with a bar above them.\n\nLet us compare what we just learned with the Mesoamerican numbers that appear in the tables of this text. To grow the count, the Etruscan-Roman numbers needed a new symbol at each important value: V for 5, X for 10, L for 50, C for 100, D for 500 and M for 1000. And, to go beyond 1000, they still needed to draw a bar above the symbols, multiplying their value by one thousand.\n\nThe Mesoamerican numbers followed the same counting pattern as the Etruscan-Roman numbers, repeating the symbol of the unit to count: a dot for 1, a bar for 5 and a sign for zero. However, because their symbols were different, they did not need to create new symbols or to make any further adjustment to represent new numbers. With only three symbols, including the zero, they kept counting and arranged the symbols in groups, one above the other. When the count reached twenty, they did not create another symbol: they simply moved to the next position, as we observed in the vigesimal tables of this text.\n\nIt is important to reinforce an idea: this does not mean that the Mesoamerican system was better or more evolved than the Etruscan-Roman one. A numerical system is not superior because it uses fewer symbols. Both peoples represented the same idea, the idea of counting; they simply found different ways to express it, each one as valuable as the other.\n\nIn the next text we will study the Roman abacus (<htcite0>). There, you will train all the knowledge associated with the Etruscan-Roman numbers, from 1 to 999,999, by moving the beads of each column.",
